;; Per-check config for the structural Clojure gates (lint-gate.*).
;;
;; THE ns-size NUMBERS ONLY EVER MOVE DOWN.
;;
;; Each :*-block value was SEEDED AT THIS TREE'S MEASURED MAXIMUM, so the gate is
;; green on arrival with ZERO exemptions and nothing may become worse than today's
;; worst. Raising one is a gate bypass in source form. Lowering one is the
;; intended direction and needs no ceremony beyond the refactor that earns it.
;;
;; The :*-warn values are a WATCHLIST: reported every run, never blocking, so the
;; distance between today's worst and where the bar should be stays visible
;; instead of a seeded ceiling quietly redefining the current maximum as fine.
;;
;; WHY EACH NUMBER CARRIES ITS PROVENANCE. Without the measurement beside it, a
;; seeded ceiling and an arbitrary one are indistinguishable — and the next author
;; raises it. `.claude/rules/gate-enforcement.md` §1 makes that a condition of a
;; metric ratchet being legitimate at all.
{:ns-size
 {;; Measured maximum: tools/renderer-gen/src/lvgl_codegen/fixtures.clj at 950
  ;; code-LOC. BLOCK is strictly-greater-than, so that file sits AT the ceiling
  ;; rather than over it.
  ;;
  ;; RE-SEEDED DOWNWARD from 1219, by a METRIC FIX rather than a refactor.
  ;; `code-loc` used to count DOCSTRINGS as code, which put two gates in direct
  ;; conflict: `lint-docstrings` requires a docstring on every function in an
  ;; enrolled root, and this ceiling then charged the namespace for it — so
  ;; satisfying one gate could break the other. IT DID: adding the docstrings this
  ;; repo's own gate demanded pushed the then-largest namespace 39 lines past a
  ;; ceiling seeded at its own previous size.
  ;;
  ;; Excluding prose is what "narration is FREE" always claimed and did not do.
  ;; Measured: palette_ladder.clj is 1258 raw and carries 442 docstring lines, so it
  ;; drops to 816 net — from the largest namespace in the tree to the fifth. The old
  ;; number was ranking DOCUMENTATION, not behaviour.
  ;;
  ;; A ceiling only ever moves DOWN, and this is down.
  :loc-block 950

  ;; Measured maximum: tools/renderer-gen/src/lvgl_codegen/proto_schema.clj at
  ;; 66 public vars.
  :publics-block 66

  ;; Watchlist thresholds, adopted from the surveyed donor repository verbatim
  ;; rather than invented here.
  :loc-warn 400
  :publics-warn 25}

 ;; ── fn-size ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; Function-grain size, three axes, same two-tier ratchet as :ns-size above.
 ;; Measured by `lint-gate.fnsize`, which is the SAME code this gate runs — so a
 ;; seeded ceiling cannot disagree with the gate enforcing it. Population: 1483
 ;; functions in 175 files across every gated root, generated projections
 ;; excluded, 0 unparseable.
 :fn-size
 {;; Measured maximum: `brief-md` at tools/devcards/src/devcards/standard_brief.clj:312
  ;; — 175 code lines over rows 312-508 (185 non-blank non-comment, less a 10-line
  ;; docstring). It is a flat string-assembly function: nesting 0, decisions 0.
  :loc-block 175

  ;; Measured maximum: `parse-frontmatter` at tools/lint/src/lint_gate/md.clj:394.
  ;; Its chain is if-not -> if-let -> fn -> cond -> if -> fn -> if, counted by hand.
  :nesting-block 7

  ;; Measured maximum: `emit-widget` at
  ;; tools/renderer-gen/src/lvgl_codegen/emit_proto.clj:435 — a 71-arm dispatch.
  ;; This is the number a HEAD-counting metric scores as 1, which is exactly why
  ;; `lint-gate.fnsize/decisions` counts arms instead.
  :decisions-block 71

  ;; ── the watchlist. Non-blocking, reported every run. ──
  ;;
  ;; :loc-warn is the donor repository's own bound and IS comparable — lines are
  ;; lines. One disclosure: this metric subtracts docstrings and the donor's does
  ;; not, so at equal prose this reads slightly lower. 19 functions sit over it.
  :loc-warn 60

  ;; :nesting-warn is THIS TREE'S p99, NOT the donor's 5, even though the two
  ;; numbers coincide — and the coincidence is the trap worth naming. The donor
  ;; counts `do`, `let` and `fn` as nesting heads; this metric counts branching and
  ;; iteration only. Same digit, different quantity, so borrowing it would have
  ;; been wrong for a reason no reader could have seen from the number.
  :nesting-warn 5

  ;; :decisions-warn is McCabe's own moderate-risk bar of 10, mapped onto this
  ;; metric: cyclomatic complexity is decision outcomes PLUS ONE, so CC 10 is 9
  ;; decisions. It is comparable here precisely because this metric counts ARMS,
  ;; which is the quantity McCabe calibrated 10 against — the donor applies that
  ;; same 10 to a HEAD count, which measures something else and under-reports.
  :decisions-warn 9}

 ;; ── docstrings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; `:enrolled` is a DECLARED SCOPE, not a baseline, and the difference is what
 ;; makes this check legitimate at all under .claude/rules/gate-enforcement.md §1:
 ;; a list of individual grandfathered findings is refused, while declaring WHICH
 ;; POPULATION a check judges is permitted PROVIDED the check is total inside it.
 ;; So every function under an enrolled root owes a docstring, with zero parked
 ;; findings — and a root that is not ready is left OUT here, by name, with the
 ;; reason, rather than enrolled with a tolerated miss count.
 ;;
 ;; NOT ENROLLED, and each for its own reason rather than for convenience:
 ;;   tools/devcards/src      the largest population still owing docstrings. A
 ;;                           real project, measured, and not a scope question —
 ;;                           it is enrolled the day it is clean.
 ;;   tools/devcards/dev      research probes. Their value is the measurement, not
 ;;   tools/renderer-gen/dev  an API a reader navigates.
 ;;   */test                  a test is a VERIFICATION surface, not a contract
 ;;                           surface: its name and its assertions ARE its
 ;;                           documentation, and a docstring on a fixture adds
 ;;                           noise without catching drift.
 :docstrings
 {:enrolled ["docs/.protodoc/tools/src"
             "tools/renderer-gen/src"
             "tools/lint/src"]}

 ;; ── spec-shape ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; Enrolled where arrow specs are actually PRACTISED. Every other gated tree
 ;; carries no `m/=>` at all, so enrolling it would add a vacuous root — and the
 ;; check's own non-vacuity floor is over SPECS, so an enrolled root with none
 ;; contributes nothing but a chance for the floor to be satisfied by a neighbour.
 ;; tools/lint/src is enrolled so this gate's own specs, if it ever grows any, are
 ;; judged by it.
 :spec-shape
 {:enrolled ["tools/renderer-gen/src"
             "docs/.protodoc/tools/src"
             "tools/lint/src"]}}
